# Stream Model Output

## Overview

Your support UI should show the answer while the model writes it, not after the
request finishes. Set `stream: true` on a ReAct request and pass a dispatch
target in the command context. Jido AI publishes each model chunk as a Signal:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
request = MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{input: "Where is order A-1001?", stream: true})

{:ok, _agent, [reply]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request, context: %{jido_ai_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}})

# Your mailbox now holds:
# jido.ai.model.stream.started    sequence 0
# jido.ai.model.stream.chunk      sequence 1..4
# jido.ai.model.stream.completed  sequence 5
```

This notebook runs one streamed request, reads the Signals, matches them to the
reply, runs the same request on a live Agent Server, and handles a failed
stream. It runs offline.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Offline Model

With `stream: true`, Jido AI calls `ReqLLM.stream_text/3`. ReqLLM streams over
Finch, not Req, so a `Req.Test` plug can't answer it. This cell starts a small
HTTP server on `127.0.0.1` that replies with OpenAI Responses API server-sent
events. ReqLLM parses them exactly as it parses OpenAI's.

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.OfflineOpenAI do
  @moduledoc "Serves one canned HTTP response to every connection."

  def start(response) do
    {:ok, listen} = :gen_tcp.listen(0, [:binary, active: false, reuseaddr: true])
    {:ok, port} = :inet.port(listen)
    spawn(fn -> serve(listen, response) end)
    "http://127.0.0.1:#{port}/v1"
  end

  def sse(deltas) do
    completed = %{
      "type" => "response.completed",
      "response" => %{
        "id" => "resp_offline",
        "status" => "completed",
        "output" => [
          %{
            "type" => "message",
            "role" => "assistant",
            "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => Enum.join(deltas)}]
          }
        ],
        "usage" => %{"input_tokens" => 12, "output_tokens" => 6, "total_tokens" => 18}
      }
    }

    events = Enum.map(deltas, &%{"type" => "response.output_text.delta", "delta" => &1})

    body =
      Enum.map_join(events ++ [completed], fn event ->
        "event: #{event["type"]}\ndata: #{Jason.encode!(event)}\n\n"
      end)

    http(200, "text/event-stream", body)
  end

  def error(status, message) do
    http(status, "application/json", Jason.encode!(%{"error" => %{"message" => message}}))
  end

  defp http(status, type, body) do
    "HTTP/1.1 #{status} X\r\ncontent-type: #{type}\r\ncontent-length: #{byte_size(body)}\r\n" <>
      "connection: close\r\n\r\n" <> body
  end

  defp serve(listen, response) do
    {:ok, socket} = :gen_tcp.accept(listen)
    :ok = read_request(socket, "")
    :ok = :gen_tcp.send(socket, response)
    :gen_tcp.close(socket)
    serve(listen, response)
  end

  defp read_request(socket, buffer) do
    with [head, body] <- String.split(buffer, "\r\n\r\n", parts: 2),
         [_, length] <- Regex.run(~r/content-length: (\d+)/i, head),
         true <- byte_size(body) >= String.to_integer(length) do
      :ok
    else
      _incomplete ->
        {:ok, data} = :gen_tcp.recv(socket, 0, 5_000)
        read_request(socket, buffer <> data)
    end
  end
end

base_url = MyApp.OfflineOpenAI.start(MyApp.OfflineOpenAI.sse(["Order A-1001 ", "shipped on ", "October 30."]))

offline = %{api_key: "offline-test-key", base_url: base_url, max_retries: 0}
```

The model streams three text deltas. `offline` is the request `options` map.
`base_url` points ReqLLM at the local server. Remove `api_key` and `base_url`
to stream from OpenAI.

## Define The Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "support_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
      instructions "Answer order questions in one sentence."
    end
  end
end
```

`stream` is a field of the `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct` input schema, with a
default of `false`. The other built-in reasoning Flows don't define it and
reject it as an unrecognized key.

## Stream A Request

`jido_ai_dispatch` is a `Jido.Signal.Dispatch` config. Jido AI dispatches
every stream Signal to it. `{:pid, target: self()}` sends each one to this
notebook as `{:signal, signal}`:

```elixir
agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    stream: true,
    options: offline
  })

{:ok, agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request, context: %{jido_ai_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}})

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output}
```

The result is `{:completed, "Order A-1001 shipped on October 30."}`.

The stream Signals are already in the mailbox. Jido AI dispatches them while
the model call runs, so all of them arrive before `cmd/3` returns.

## Read The Signals

```elixir
drain = fn drain, signals ->
  receive do
    {:signal, signal} -> drain.(drain, [signal | signals])
  after
    0 -> Enum.reverse(signals)
  end
end

signals = drain.(drain, [])

Enum.map(signals, &{&1.type, &1.data.sequence})
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  {"jido.ai.model.stream.started", 0},
  {"jido.ai.model.stream.chunk", 1},
  {"jido.ai.model.stream.chunk", 2},
  {"jido.ai.model.stream.chunk", 3},
  {"jido.ai.model.stream.chunk", 4},
  {"jido.ai.model.stream.completed", 5}
]
```

| Type | When | Extra data |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `jido.ai.model.stream.started` | Before ReqLLM opens the stream | none |
| `jido.ai.model.stream.chunk` | Once per ReqLLM chunk | `chunk` |
| `jido.ai.model.stream.completed` | After the last chunk | `response_id`, `outcome`, `finish_reason` |
| `jido.ai.model.stream.failed` | When the stream can't open or breaks | `reason` |

`sequence` starts at `0` and increases by one per Signal within one model call.
Use it to order or deduplicate Signals at the receiver.

## Read The Chunks

Each `chunk` is a portable map with `type`, `text`, `name`, `arguments`, and
`metadata`:

```elixir
signals
|> Enum.filter(&(&1.type == "jido.ai.model.stream.chunk"))
|> Enum.map(&{&1.data.chunk.type, &1.data.chunk.text})
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  {:content, "Order A-1001 "},
  {:content, "shipped on "},
  {:content, "October 30."},
  {:meta, nil}
]
```

`:content` chunks carry text. The final `:meta` chunk carries ReqLLM's usage,
`finish_reason`, and `response_id` in `metadata`. ReqLLM also emits
`:thinking` chunks for reasoning text and `:tool_call` chunks with `name` and
`arguments`.

## Match The Reply

Every stream Signal carries the same correlation fields as the reply:

```elixir
completed = List.last(signals)

Map.take(completed.data, [:request_id, :session_id, :thread_id, :model, :model_call, :outcome])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  request_id: "01a17d76-1bd9-7921-a3b0-2ced49779eba",
  session_id: "session:support-1",
  thread_id: "session:support-1:thread",
  model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini",
  model_call: 1,
  outcome: :final_answer
}
```

Your `request_id` is a different generated ID. It is the request Signal's `id`:

```elixir
{completed.data.request_id == request.id, completed.data.request_id == reply.data.request_id,
 completed.data.run_id == reply.data.run_id}
```

The result is `{true, true, true}`.

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `request_id` | The request Signal ID. Same on the reply. |
| `run_id` | One run of the reasoning Flow. Same on the reply. |
| `session_id`, `thread_id` | The conversation that receives the answer. |
| `stream_id` | One model call's stream. New for every model call. |
| `model`, `model_index` | The model used and its position in `[model \| fallback_models]`. |
| `model_call` | The request's model call count, starting at `1`. |
| `model_attempt` | The attempt number on this model, starting at `1`. |
| `tool_rounds` | Tool rounds finished before this model call. |
| `sequence`, `timestamp_ms` | Order and wall-clock time. |

Each stream Signal also has the Agent's `signal_source` as `source` and the
request ID in its `jidocausationid` extension:

```elixir
{completed.source, Jido.Signal.get_context(completed, "jidocausationid") == request.id}
```

The result is `{"/my_app/support_agent", true}`.

## Stream And Reply

The stream is a preview. The reply and the Session are the record:

```elixir
streamed =
  signals
  |> Enum.filter(&(&1.type == "jido.ai.model.stream.chunk" and &1.data.chunk.type == :content))
  |> Enum.map_join(& &1.data.chunk.text)

assistant_messages =
  Enum.filter(agent.state.session.thread.entries, &(&1.payload["role"] == "assistant"))

{streamed == reply.data.output, length(assistant_messages)}
```

The result is `{true, 1}`. ReqLLM collects the chunks into one response. Jido
AI then runs the output guard and appends one assistant message to the Thread.
The reply has the same fields as a non-streamed reply.

A request with tool rounds streams once per model call. Each call gets a new
`stream_id` and `model_call`, and `tool_rounds` tells you how many tool rounds
came before it. Only the last call's `completed` Signal has
`outcome: :final_answer`; earlier ones have `outcome: :tool_calls`. A retry or
fallback also starts a new stream, so a UI should reset its text buffer on
every `started` Signal.

## Stream On A Server

`Jido.AgentServer.call/3` passes `context:` to the Turn. Use it the same way:

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(MyApp.SupportAgent, id: "live-support")

live_request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    stream: true,
    options: offline
  })

{:ok, _committed, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: live_reply}]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(server, live_request,
    context: %{jido_ai_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}}
  )

live_signals = drain.(drain, [])

{live_reply.data.output, Enum.map(live_signals, & &1.data.sequence)}
```

The result is `{"Order A-1001 shipped on October 30.", [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]}`.
The context isn't stored in Agent state or Signal data. Pass it on each call
that should stream to a receiver.

Without `jido_ai_dispatch`, the model call still streams and the reply is the
same, but Jido AI publishes no stream Signals.

## A Failed Stream

Point a second server at a `401` response:

```elixir
failing_url = MyApp.OfflineOpenAI.start(MyApp.OfflineOpenAI.error(401, "Incorrect API key provided"))

failing =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    stream: true,
    options: %{offline | base_url: failing_url}
  })

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: failed}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-2"), failing,
    context: %{jido_ai_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}}
  )

{failed.data.status, failed.data.reason_code, Enum.map(drain.(drain, []), & &1.type)}
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:failed, :model, ["jido.ai.model.stream.started", "jido.ai.model.stream.failed"]}
```

The `failed` Signal has `sequence: 1` and the error text in `reason`. A `401`
isn't retryable, so the request fails without a retry. ReqLLM also logs a
warning. For a retryable error such as a `503`, ReAct retries or moves to a
fallback model, and each attempt sends its own `started` Signal.

## Clean Up

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("live-support")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- Set `stream: true` on a ReAct request and pass `jido_ai_dispatch` in the
  command or Agent Server context.
- Each model call publishes `started`, one `chunk` per ReqLLM chunk, then
  `completed` or `failed`.
- `sequence` orders Signals within one `stream_id`.
- `request_id`, `run_id`, `session_id`, and `thread_id` match the reply.
- The reply and the single committed assistant message are the final record.

